{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_015uiLvXjFykvUxU4Dq1hpeu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"One test fails: `invalid_group`. The issue is:\n- GNU gchown includes the colon in the group name: `'chown: invalid group: ':nonexistent_group_xyz'`\n- Our binary strips the colon: `chown: invalid group: 'nonexistent_group_xyz'`\n\nGNU chown passes the full spec `:nonexistent_group_xyz` to the error, while our code parses the spec first and only passes the group name part to `lookupGroup`.\n\nI need to include the colon prefix in the error message when the owner spec starts with `:`. \n\n\nThe cleanest approach is to have `parseOwnerSpec` catch the error from `lookupGroup` and re-throw it with the original spec included, so the error message preserves the colon that was in the user's input. The real issue is that GNU chown includes the full spec with the colon in its error message, so I need to pass that context through to lookupGroup somehow. The cleanest approach would be to add an optional parameter to lookupGroup for the error label, or restructure parseOwnerSpec to handle the error formatting itself since it has access to the original input. I could pass the original spec string to the lookup function so it can use the exact text in error messages—that way `:nonexistent_group_xyz` would appear in the error rather than just `nonexistent_group_xyz`. The question is whether GNU chown includes the full spec like `user:badgroup` or just the group part when reporting errors. I'm realizing the cleanest approach is to pass the original spec string down to the lookup functions so they can include it in error messages. Rather than restructuring the whole function, I'll just modify lookupGroup to accept the spec parameter for proper error formatting. I'm realizing GNU chown probably just uses whatever group portion appears in the original spec string—so for `:nonexistent_group_xyz`, it includes the colon